e evaluation reproduces the ~39 percentage point delta that validates the headline comparison, and these four claims form a coherent logical chain.\n\nThe narrative flows naturally from detection through analysis to negative results and intervention, which fits within 8 pages. Now I'm working through predictions for each planned and orphaned experiment, starting with the compute-waste-audit. This retrospective analysis examines stagnation tails in existing trajectory data—SWE-Agent shows roughly 28% stagnation rate across 54k windows while OpenHands sits around 21% across 150k windows. Resolved trajectories have only 1.3% error rate and are nearly immune to false termination, whereas unresolved trajectories drive most errors. For the best tier, stagnation tail compute percentage should fall between 35-55%, since unresolved trajectories typically enter stagnation midway through execution where the agent wastes substantial computation on ineffective actions.\n\nFor the early-termination-simulation experiment, I'm modeling what happens when we apply consecutive-k stagnation thresholds to trigger early stopping. The best tier should preserve 93-98% of task success while saving 25-40% of compute, with different k values producing different tradeoffs between false termination risk and efficiency gains.\n\nSince the structural GB model has such strong cross-agent performance and resolved trajectories show minimal error rates, the false kill rate should stay quite low even for resolved cases. Then I'm looking at the cost-effectiveness Pareto frontier, which should land somewhere between 0.78-0.9 normalized AUC depending on how these parameters interact.\n\nFor the 2-LF cross-agent configuration without OB, the training data shows perfect agreement between 2-LF and 3-LF labels when confidence is at least 2, and the own test achieves 0.951 macro F1, so I'm evaluating how this deployable config performs across different agents.\n\nNow I'm examining the structural model performance on high-confidence OH data—both RF and GB exceed 0.99—and trying to understand whether the 2-LF version maintains that same level of cross-agent transfer. For the matched-scope evaluation, I need to account for the fact that the probe was tested on a different split than the structural GB model, which creates roughly a 37-42 percentage point gap in performance.\n\nLooking at the LLM-as-judge results on OpenHands, Claude achieves around 0.66 zero-shot and up to 0.80 with few-shot chain-of-thought, which would likely hold or degrade slightly when applied cross-agent. This would further demonstrate the structural approach's advantage. Finally, I need to evaluate GB's performance on the SWE gold standard dataset. I'm seeing the registry entries for the PREPARING experiments now. The orphaned plan items map to these: 2lf-cross-agent corresponds to e1-2lf-crossagent, matched-scope-eval maps to e2-gb-matched-scope, and there's also e4-gb-swe-gold which doesn't have a matching plan entry. I need to work through predictions for all of these experiments with detailed reasoning.\n\nStarting with compute-waste-audit: I'm looking at the stagnation tail data across SWE-Agent and OH. SWE-Agent shows about 1683 instances with a 28.46% stagnation rate, while OH has roughly 3409 instances at 21.45%. Since most trajectories remain unresolved (75-80%), the agent typically hits terminal stagnation partway through and continues burning compute until hitting max_steps. Based on this pattern, I'm estimating the stagnation tail compute percentage at around 45% for SWE-Agent and 42% for OH, which falls within the expected 35-55% range for the best tier.\n\nFor the token analysis, stagnation windows likely consume more tokens than progress windows since agents in stagnation tend to produce verbose error messages and repeat commands. Now I'm setting up the early-termination-simulation with a parameter sweep across consecutive_k values from 1 to 10, where k=1 is aggressive but risks false positives, k=3 is moderate, k=5 is conservative, and k=7+ is very conservative. The structural GB cross-agent F1 score of 0.967 overall and 0.992 at confidence ≥2 gives me a strong baseline for evaluating how well early termination can catch stagnation without breaking resolved trajectories.\n\nLooking at the performance metrics, k=3 emerges as the sweet spot—it saves about 33% of compute while preserving 96% of task success. k=5 is more conservative, saving 28% compute but preserving 98% success, while k=1 is aggressive at 42% compute savings but dips to 91% success preservation. The false positive rate is the main concern since early termination risks killing trajectories that would eventually resolve. When I map this into a Pareto curve combining compute savings against task success preservation, the structural detector's curve should dominate random baselines and approach oracle performance, with a normalized AUC between 0.78 and 0.9.\n\nNow looking at the cross-agent validation, the 2-LF model trained on SWE-Agent labels and tested on OpenHands labels shows strong agreement where both labeling functions agree (confidence ≥2), with an expected macro F1 of 0.978 falling comfortably within the best tier range of 0.965 to 0.985.\n\nFor the matched-scope evaluation, I'm comparing structural GB and the probe on the same OpenHands subset. The existing data shows GB at 0.9674 overall and the probe at 0.5984 zero-shot, but the matched scope likely uses the confidence ≥2 subset where GB reaches ~0.992 and the probe stays around 0.598, giving a delta of approximately 39.4 percentage points, which aligns with the plan's stated +39.4pp difference and fits within the predicted range of 37-42pp.\n\nFor LLM-as-judge on OpenHands, I'm extrapolating from SWE-Agent performance where Claude achieves 0.799 with few-shot chain-of-thought and 0.663 zero-shot. Cross-agent evaluation on OpenHands would likely perform slightly worse due to format differences, so I'd estimate Claude zero