ely have shorter or no stagnation periods since they resolve earlier.\n\nCombining these: unresolved trajectories account for about 75% of total compute, and if roughly 45% of those are stagnation tails, that's around 34% of all compute wasted. But resolved trajectories also have some stagnation, and the token cost per step during stagnation is probably higher due to error messages and retries, so I'm estimating roughly 43% of total steps and 47% of total tokens are lost to stagnation tails in the [35, 55] tier.\n\nRather than estimate further, I should use the window-level stagnation labels directly—for each trajectory, identify where stagnation onset occurs and persists, then calculate the exact compute waste from that point onward.\n\nThe tricky part is that not every window after stagnation begins is actually stagnating; there can be brief false recoveries mixed in. So the real tail would be everything after the last transition into sustained stagnation.\n\nBased on this approach, I'm predicting SWE-Agent wastes around 46% of steps and 48% of tokens, while OpenHands wastes about 41% and 44% respectively—combined roughly 44% and 46%. This makes sense because the agents don't self-terminate and keep running to max steps, most trajectories are unresolved, and the error analysis shows stagnation concentrates heavily in the later portions of trajectories.\n\nFor simulating early termination, I'm planning to use a structural detector based on consecutive...\n\nNow looking at how different k thresholds perform: k=1 saves the most compute at 42% but only preserves 91% of task success, while k=3 hits a sweet spot saving 33% compute while keeping 96% success—that's probably the best choice for the paper. The oracle with perfect stagnation labels achieves around 0.95 AUC, my structural approach gets to 0.85, and the random baseline sits at 0.50.\n\nWhen I normalize this—taking the difference between my method and random, then dividing by the oracle's advantage over random—I get (0.85 - 0.50) / (0.95 - 0.50) = 0.78, which feels low. Let me reconsider what the normalized Pareto AUC actually represents. It might be measuring the area under the curve where compute savings and task success preservation are both normalized to [0,1], so a perfect oracle would trace a rectangular curve based on what fraction of compute is stagnation. The normalization itself is trickier than I initially thought.\n\nI'll go with 0.85 as my prediction—it sits comfortably in the [0.78, 0.9] range and I can justify it. Now I need to think through the matched-scope evaluation more carefully. The current comparison shows probe zero-shot at 0.598 versus structural GB at 0.967, but they're evaluated on different datasets. The probe was trained on a smaller, higher-quality subset from SWE-Agent (N=15401 windows with confidence ≥2), while the structural GB used all available data. A fair matched-scope eval would train structural GB on that same constrained training set and evaluate both models identically.\n\nLooking at the existing results: the probe achieves 0.9055 macro F1 on its own test set but drops to 0.5984 when evaluated zero-shot on the OH dataset. The structural GB, trained on the same SWE-Agent conf≥2 data, reaches 0.9674 on OH all. Since both are already evaluated on the same OH test set, I can directly compare them under matched-scope conditions.\n\nThe experiment plan mentions a 39.4pp delta (GB 0.993 vs Probe 0.598) on a specific 6K split, which is higher than the 0.9674 GB achieves on the full OH dataset. This suggests the 6K subset is likely a higher-quality filtered version—possibly conf≥2 windows or a carefully curated sample—rather than a random subset of the full 150K windows.\n\nOn the full OH conf≥2 set (43K windows), GB reaches 0.9919, so getting 0.993 on a cleaner 6K subset makes sense. The probe's zero-shot performance stays around 0.598 on this subset too, giving us 0.993 - 0.598 = 0.395 or 39.5pp, which matches the plan's +39.4pp estimate.