ow Claude at 0.6625 zero-shot and 0.7994 with few-shot chain-of-thought, while GPT-4.5 hits 0.7465 zero-shot. But OH uses a completely different command structure and output format, so those few-shot examples from SWE-Agent won't transfer cleanly. I'd expect Claude zero-shot to drop to around 0.63 on OH, few-shot to land around 0.72, and the best LLM performance overall to reach approximately 0.75. This reinforces that GB's structural advantage holds across different agent types.\n\nNow I'm ready to compile the full YAML output with all the imagined experiments and detailed predictions.\n\nGenerating YAML structure... I want to make sure the numbers are internally consistent. For example, the early termination savings should be consistent with the compute waste audit findings.\n\nIf stagnation tail is ~45% of compute, a perfect oracle could save ~45% while preserving task success, but since resolved trajectories rarely enter sustained stagnation (error rate 1.3%), a detector with k=3 consecutive windows would catch stagnation onset with minimal delay and false terminations. With a realistic recall of ~0.95, the actual compute saved would be around 40% after accounting for detection delay and missed stagnation windows. Looking at the actual trajectory lengths from the data, I'm realizing my estimate might be off. With around 163 steps per trajectory on average and sliding windows of 5 steps each, the savings calculation changes significantly. Let me reconsider how the window overlap affects the actual step count and whether my 8-step savings per trajectory is realistic given these longer trajectories. the stride is 1 step, then num_windows ≈ num_steps - 4. So ~32.6 windows ≈ ~37 steps per trajectory on average.\n\nHmm, for OH: 150652 windows from 3409 instances = ~44 windows per instance ≈ ~48 steps per trajectory.\n\nWith trajectories averaging ~37-48 steps:\n- Stagnation onset at ~50-60% through → ~20-28 steps in\n- Tail length: ~15-20 steps\n- With k=3 consecutive