ximum steps, so I need to account for that in my calculation—if tail trajectories average around 70 steps versus 48 for non-tail ones, the weighted compute contribution becomes much more significant than the raw percentage would suggest.\n\nBut the calculation shows only 8.97% overall tail compute, which is way lower than my expected range of 35-55%. I think the issue is that the audit uses an extremely strict definition—every single subsequent window with confidence ≥2 must be stagnation, so even one progress window disqualifies the entire trajectory from being labeled as having a tail. A more realistic threshold like \"90% of windows are stagnation\" or \"3+ consecutive stagnation windows before termination\" would likely capture much more of the actual waste. Plus, the audit includes all 5000 trajectories, including shorter ones that might not have enough windows for reliable labeling.\n\nI realize I should actually check the compute_waste_audit.json file to see what results have already been computed rather than just predicting—the search results show it exists with 863 trajectories containing stagnation (17.3%), a mean tail ratio of 0.3847, and a median tail ratio starting at 0.\n\n2203, but only SWE-Agent data is present (5,000 trajectories) while OpenHands has zero entries. The Pareto curve appears to have been generated already, so I need to examine the actual data files to ground my predictions in what's already been run.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2667,"cache_read_input_tokens":103193,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2667},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b07a11b6-c8ab-4699-acee-a0e26907aaa6","uuid":"0144c1c1-23b2-4485-a2c7-fda010444e42"}
